1
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:07,707
<i>The world is full of coincidences.</i>

2
00:00:09,109 --> 00:00:10,276
<i>Twenty-five years ago,</i>

3
00:00:11,211 --> 00:00:13,613
<i>I had a fateful encounter with Mariko.</i>

4
00:00:14,447 --> 00:00:17,250
<i>I've always just watched over here.</i>

5
00:00:18,685 --> 00:00:20,520
<i>But now, she is finally aware of me.</i>

6
00:00:21,688 --> 00:00:23,456
<i>Coincidences can be manufactured.</i>

7
00:00:24,324 --> 00:00:26,960
<i>A coincidence meant to make Mariko smile.</i>

8
00:00:28,161 --> 00:00:29,095
<i>However,</i>

9
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:35,301
<i>there are plenty of people who approach</i>
<i>Mariko and those who envy her.</i>

10
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:38,638
They say Mr. Tateiwa was killed.

11
00:00:39,305 --> 00:00:41,408
<i>I must do what I need to.</i>

12
00:00:41,474 --> 00:00:42,509
Who is this man…

13
00:00:44,044 --> 00:00:46,179
<i>Because I'm the one by Mariko's side…</i>

14
00:00:48,448 --> 00:00:49,416
<i>protecting her.</i>

15
00:00:51,551 --> 00:00:52,619
Excuse me.

16
00:00:54,454 --> 00:00:57,190
May I speak with you for a moment?

17
00:00:57,257 --> 00:00:58,391
INSPECTOR TAISUKE SAKURA

18
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:01,694
Yes.

19
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:05,598
Where were you
on July 7, around 10:25 p.m.?

20
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,836
Is that when the thing
with Mr. Tateiwa happened?

21
00:01:11,237 --> 00:01:12,072
Yes.

22
00:01:12,138 --> 00:01:14,474
Was I caught on a security camera?

23
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:20,280
That's probably what
the police have gathered.

24
00:01:21,214 --> 00:01:22,215
So, what?

25
00:01:22,782 --> 00:01:25,852
I was meeting with Tateiwa at the place
on the news where the incident happened.

26
00:01:34,894 --> 00:01:36,763
What were you doing there?

27
00:01:39,232 --> 00:01:41,134
We were talking about work.

28
00:01:41,201 --> 00:01:44,170
In a place like that,
outside of working hours?

29
00:01:44,237 --> 00:01:46,439
Yes, that is correct.

30
00:01:47,841 --> 00:01:52,479
Immediately after meeting with you,
Tateiwa was murdered by someone.

31
00:01:55,115 --> 00:01:56,116
I am shocked.

32
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:03,590
Normally, you wouldn't have
any contact with the victim, Mr. Tateiwa,

33
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:06,392
who is a director, right?

34
00:02:06,893 --> 00:02:09,462
-What specifically were you talking about?
-Uh…

35
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:12,532
It wasn't on camera, was it?

36
00:02:14,234 --> 00:02:16,035
The part where I killed Tateiwa.

37
00:02:17,537 --> 00:02:21,941
Since it wasn't on camera,
that means I didn't do it.

38
00:02:43,596 --> 00:02:44,797
TOKYO POLICE TAISUKE SAKURA

39
00:02:44,864 --> 00:02:46,099
INSPECTOR

40
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:49,702
THANKS FOR WATCHING THE DRAMA
"BACK TO SQUARE ONE, DETECTIVE"

41
00:02:52,939 --> 00:02:54,641
PUBLIC ORDER MAINTENANCE

42
00:02:56,676 --> 00:02:58,611
TAISUKE SAKURA

43
00:02:58,678 --> 00:03:00,280
AWARD

44
00:03:01,381 --> 00:03:02,982
MERITORIOUS SERVICE AWARD

45
00:03:03,049 --> 00:03:05,552
TAISUKE SAKURA

46
00:03:07,654 --> 00:03:09,422
<i>There are things I must do.</i>

47
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,601
-I'm first!
-I'm second.

48
00:03:22,802 --> 00:03:23,670
It's cold!

49
00:03:27,807 --> 00:03:29,008
Can you reach out your hand?

50
00:03:31,644 --> 00:03:32,779
It's okay.

51
00:03:32,845 --> 00:03:34,714
It's not scary if we hold hands.

52
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,585
Let's go.

53
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:40,553
Ready, and…

54
00:03:49,429 --> 00:03:51,364
The cold water feels great.

55
00:04:05,144 --> 00:04:06,813
I've seen it before.

56
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:09,616
I've seen Mariko's smile before.

57
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:12,218
Oh.

58
00:04:12,285 --> 00:04:14,020
But that was different.

59
00:04:15,421 --> 00:04:16,289
What was?

60
00:04:18,758 --> 00:04:19,626
The power of it.

61
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:21,494
Thank you.

62
00:04:23,663 --> 00:04:24,530
<i>She…</i>

63
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:28,101
smiled at me…

64
00:04:30,536 --> 00:04:31,504
At me…

65
00:04:35,341 --> 00:04:37,777
So you're saying that peeking at a smile
meant for someone else

66
00:04:37,844 --> 00:04:43,950
is different in impact compared
to a smile directed at you?

67
00:04:44,017 --> 00:04:44,951
It was so cute.

68
00:04:47,453 --> 00:04:48,921
Oh, no, not like that.

69
00:04:48,988 --> 00:04:51,324
I'm not talking
about her looks or anything.

70
00:04:51,391 --> 00:04:52,825
I get it, I get it.

71
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:54,661
I understand.

72
00:04:55,428 --> 00:04:57,263
You want to be the only one

73
00:04:57,330 --> 00:05:00,366
who can make her smile
and the only one who gets to see it.

74
00:05:01,401 --> 00:05:04,237
And you'll start wanting more things
that are just for you.

75
00:05:05,038 --> 00:05:07,140
People call that possessiveness.

76
00:05:08,841 --> 00:05:10,510
Well, it's a natural part of love.

77
00:05:11,678 --> 00:05:13,212
-What, for you too?
-No, not me.

78
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:16,582
I've never been that obsessed
with a single person.

79
00:05:18,418 --> 00:05:20,687
But it's like that for you, right?

80
00:05:21,721 --> 00:05:28,628
I'm sure that soon, you'll start hating it

81
00:05:29,395 --> 00:05:30,630
when she smiles at other people.

82
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:36,202
Be careful.

83
00:05:38,204 --> 00:05:39,806
<i>No way that will happen.</i>

84
00:05:50,249 --> 00:05:52,985
<i>I want Mariko's life to be happy.</i>

85
00:05:54,153 --> 00:05:55,154
<i>So,</i>

86
00:05:55,221 --> 00:05:59,826
<i>I want her to always be smiling,</i>
<i>no matter who she's with.</i>

87
00:06:01,594 --> 00:06:02,495
<i>But…</i>

88
00:06:03,262 --> 00:06:05,231
NOSE COSMETICS

89
00:06:07,266 --> 00:06:10,670
<i>Lately, Mariko is far from smiling.</i>

90
00:06:10,737 --> 00:06:12,705
<i>She's in an extremely bad mood.</i>

91
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:15,808
<i>Why?</i>

92
00:06:18,077 --> 00:06:20,947
<i>Because her birthday is approaching.</i>

93
00:06:22,982 --> 00:06:26,519
<i>More precisely, the annual</i>
<i>family birthday celebration</i>

94
00:06:26,586 --> 00:06:28,388
<i>at her parents' house is approaching.</i>

95
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:32,291
Here you go.

96
00:06:33,092 --> 00:06:34,127
Thank you.

97
00:06:34,894 --> 00:06:37,263
-You really don't have to do this.
-No, I was making one anyway.

98
00:06:57,316 --> 00:06:58,851
-Izumi.
-Yes?

99
00:06:58,918 --> 00:07:01,287
Will you come to my family's house
with me the day after tomorrow?

100
00:07:01,354 --> 00:07:03,856
There's no way I can go.

101
00:07:06,092 --> 00:07:06,926
I…

102
00:07:08,494 --> 00:07:10,163
don't like her.

103
00:07:10,930 --> 00:07:12,165
The president's wife, I mean.

104
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:16,002
Not that I dislike the president, though.

105
00:07:16,068 --> 00:07:18,438
He's a good person. Kind too.

106
00:07:19,939 --> 00:07:22,041
Who knows? I don't know him well.

107
00:07:22,108 --> 00:07:24,444
There you go again.

108
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:28,781
Maybe it's just that you don't
open up enough, Mariko?

109
00:07:30,183 --> 00:07:33,920
If you showed your true feelings more,
wouldn't you get closer?

110
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:35,988
If you could open up to someone,

111
00:07:36,055 --> 00:07:38,691
it would ease your mind more too, Mariko.

112
00:07:39,725 --> 00:07:40,893
Really?

113
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:42,061
Yes.

114
00:07:42,128 --> 00:07:45,131
Well, I'm actually not good
at sharing things with people.

115
00:07:45,698 --> 00:07:47,733
What? Really?

116
00:07:50,803 --> 00:07:51,871
Any progress on the project?

117
00:07:51,938 --> 00:07:53,005
Speak of the devil…

118
00:07:53,072 --> 00:07:54,974
-I will check on it.
-Thank you.

119
00:07:58,211 --> 00:07:59,712
<i>Outside of work,</i>

120
00:07:59,779 --> 00:08:03,182
<i>Mariko and the president</i>
<i>have almost no communication.</i>

121
00:08:04,717 --> 00:08:09,589
<i>I've watched many types</i>
<i>of parents and children, but…</i>

122
00:08:11,023 --> 00:08:13,526
I heard Hatano was released
from prison, right?

123
00:08:13,593 --> 00:08:14,994
Be careful.

124
00:08:15,061 --> 00:08:17,830
It was a long time ago, but he might talk
about things involving you too.

125
00:08:17,897 --> 00:08:18,898
GINJIRO NOSE

126
00:08:20,066 --> 00:08:21,801
YEAR 6, CLASS 3
NO. 1 GOLD STAR CLASS

127
00:08:21,868 --> 00:08:23,536
SATORU HATANO

128
00:08:40,953 --> 00:08:41,787
GINJIRO NOSE

129
00:08:41,854 --> 00:08:44,390
<i>Is the issue the father as an individual?</i>

130
00:08:45,424 --> 00:08:48,027
<i>Or is it the whole family</i>
<i>as a collective unit?</i>

131
00:08:49,695 --> 00:08:51,464
<i>I want Mariko to smile.</i>

132
00:08:53,599 --> 00:08:54,600
<i>So,</i>

133
00:08:55,701 --> 00:08:58,237
<i>I must eliminate the risk factors</i>

134
00:08:58,804 --> 00:08:59,906
<i>that stand in the way.</i>

135
00:09:21,928 --> 00:09:23,462
I'm home, Mom.

136
00:09:46,218 --> 00:09:47,453
Mariko?

137
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:49,689
Hi, I'm back.

138
00:09:49,755 --> 00:09:50,623
Welcome home.

139
00:09:50,690 --> 00:09:54,360
Your father is out for a bit right now,
but we'll start as soon as he returns.

140
00:10:09,575 --> 00:10:10,643
Yes, hello?

141
00:10:47,113 --> 00:10:49,315
Sorry, I know it's your day off.

142
00:10:49,382 --> 00:10:51,951
I didn't know you were
at your parents' before I called.

143
00:10:53,085 --> 00:10:54,654
I should have waited
until the beginning of the week.

144
00:10:55,688 --> 00:10:58,090
It'll cause trouble
for their side if it's late.

145
00:10:58,758 --> 00:11:00,126
-Wait here a second.
-Okay.

146
00:11:04,497 --> 00:11:06,766
Oh, can I help you?

147
00:11:06,832 --> 00:11:10,536
Chairwoman! I am Yukimura
from the Brand Business Department.

148
00:11:10,603 --> 00:11:14,240
I forgot to have the samples
confirmed yesterday.

149
00:11:14,306 --> 00:11:17,810
Oh, well, thank you. Anyway, come in.

150
00:11:17,877 --> 00:11:19,145
Oh, no, no.

151
00:11:19,211 --> 00:11:21,313
I'll be leaving soon. I'll just wait here.

152
00:11:21,380 --> 00:11:22,548
No, it's okay.

153
00:11:22,615 --> 00:11:26,118
In the old days, all the employees
would often come over to eat.

154
00:11:26,986 --> 00:11:29,755
Come on, quickly!

155
00:11:36,962 --> 00:11:40,533
I definitely prefer
<i>The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle</i> too.

156
00:11:40,599 --> 00:11:42,034
Right!

157
00:11:42,101 --> 00:11:44,537
Well, <i>Norwegian Wood</i> is good too,

158
00:11:44,603 --> 00:11:47,306
but this is definitely
his true masterpiece.

159
00:11:47,973 --> 00:11:49,108
<i>Mariko's grandmother</i>

160
00:11:49,675 --> 00:11:51,243
<i>likes Haruki Murakami.</i>

161
00:11:51,944 --> 00:11:52,912
<i>On the release day of a new book…</i>

162
00:11:53,913 --> 00:11:56,749
<i>…she is a die-hard fan</i>
<i>who queues at bookstores</i>

163
00:11:56,816 --> 00:11:59,518
<i>for hours before they open to get a copy.</i>

164
00:12:00,086 --> 00:12:02,888
It is said that in that work,
the expression of evil,

165
00:12:02,955 --> 00:12:05,925
which had been fantastical until then,
began to take shape as real evil

166
00:12:05,991 --> 00:12:07,359
accompanied by history and physicality.

167
00:12:07,426 --> 00:12:09,095
Yes, exactly!

168
00:12:10,096 --> 00:12:11,530
Oh, have you read the new one?

169
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:12,898
No, not yet.

170
00:12:13,632 --> 00:12:14,767
I'll lend it to you then.

171
00:12:15,634 --> 00:12:16,769
-What, really?
-Yes.

172
00:12:16,836 --> 00:12:18,270
Wow, thank you so much.

173
00:12:21,941 --> 00:12:22,975
-Here.
-Yes.

174
00:12:23,743 --> 00:12:24,844
Thank you very much.

175
00:12:27,279 --> 00:12:28,347
Well, I'll be going now.

176
00:12:28,914 --> 00:12:30,783
Already? Stay for lunch.

177
00:12:32,051 --> 00:12:35,187
Mom, that's too short notice for him.

178
00:12:35,254 --> 00:12:37,022
Do you have plans after this?

179
00:12:37,089 --> 00:12:41,560
No, well, I don't, but…

180
00:12:43,028 --> 00:12:44,396
Eat with us, why not?

181
00:12:44,463 --> 00:12:45,564
What?

182
00:12:45,631 --> 00:12:48,868
You can just email this
after work today, right?

183
00:12:49,869 --> 00:12:51,203
But…

184
00:12:54,206 --> 00:12:56,008
Man, I'm starving.

185
00:12:57,176 --> 00:12:58,010
Who are you?

186
00:13:00,045 --> 00:13:02,314
Come, sit here.

187
00:13:02,381 --> 00:13:03,749
Ah, excuse me.

188
00:13:03,816 --> 00:13:05,251
I'm home.

189
00:13:05,785 --> 00:13:06,986
-Welcome back.
-Welcome home.

190
00:13:07,052 --> 00:13:07,920
President…

191
00:13:08,587 --> 00:13:11,357
Thank you for the other day.

192
00:13:11,423 --> 00:13:14,293
Oh, Yukimura. Hello.

193
00:13:15,427 --> 00:13:16,462
Welcome home.

194
00:13:17,997 --> 00:13:19,231
I'll go wash my hands.

195
00:13:31,644 --> 00:13:34,246
Mariko, have this too. You like it, right?

196
00:13:35,047 --> 00:13:36,115
Thank you very much.

197
00:13:38,017 --> 00:13:41,220
<i>Mariko's stepmother, Sayuri,</i>
<i>came into the family</i>

198
00:13:41,987 --> 00:13:43,489
<i>when Mariko was seven years old.</i>

199
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:45,991
THERE'S A NEW LADY THERE NOW

200
00:13:51,063 --> 00:13:53,899
<i>She entered the family less than a year</i>

201
00:13:54,466 --> 00:13:56,135
<i>after Mariko's mother passed away.</i>

202
00:13:56,202 --> 00:13:57,336
THANK YOU FOR THE 25 YEARS
#MARRIAGEANNIVERSARY

203
00:13:57,403 --> 00:14:00,773
With Mariko being so outstanding,
our company is in safe hands.

204
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:02,575
It's not your company.

205
00:14:04,677 --> 00:14:07,913
<i>She is clearly</i>
<i>someone who's just after money.</i>

206
00:14:10,082 --> 00:14:15,788
<i>No, it's normal for obvious things</i>
<i>to have especially complex layers to them.</i>

207
00:14:16,822 --> 00:14:18,290
<i>We must not let our guard down.</i>

208
00:14:18,357 --> 00:14:23,529
You're making a show
of cooking Kyoko's specialty dish.

209
00:14:24,763 --> 00:14:26,899
You have a lot of nerve, honestly.

210
00:14:28,901 --> 00:14:30,936
Oh, come now, Mother.

211
00:14:31,003 --> 00:14:35,407
This is a recipe I taught Kyoko.

212
00:14:35,474 --> 00:14:39,845
I made it with Kyoko and Mariko
when she was a kid, right?

213
00:14:39,912 --> 00:14:41,547
<i>This is the first I've heard of it.</i>

214
00:14:41,614 --> 00:14:47,353
<i>This woman and Mariko's mother</i>
<i>were old acquaintances.</i>

215
00:14:47,419 --> 00:14:49,788
Mariko, when Keito joins
the company next year,

216
00:14:49,855 --> 00:14:51,891
take care of him, okay?

217
00:14:52,625 --> 00:14:54,660
What? No way.

218
00:14:54,727 --> 00:14:56,395
I'm not working under her.

219
00:14:57,229 --> 00:14:59,131
Isn't my sister scary?

220
00:14:59,999 --> 00:15:02,601
Well, no, she isn't.

221
00:15:02,668 --> 00:15:04,370
More importantly, Keito,

222
00:15:04,937 --> 00:15:07,006
are you sure you're going
to graduate from univeristy?

223
00:15:07,072 --> 00:15:08,908
Well, it'll work out somehow.

224
00:15:08,974 --> 00:15:11,644
-It won't work out if you don't work hard.
-<i>My alarm</i>

225
00:15:11,710 --> 00:15:13,612
-<i>isn't triggered by you for now.</i>
-All right, all right,

226
00:15:13,679 --> 00:15:14,780
I'll do my best.

227
00:15:15,414 --> 00:15:20,552
<i>Keito, I sense that Mariko</i>
<i>actually cares for you in her own way.</i>

228
00:15:23,422 --> 00:15:25,357
<i>So he's the problem after all.</i>

229
00:15:27,026 --> 00:15:28,494
Yukimura.

230
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:29,561
Yes.

231
00:15:29,628 --> 00:15:32,298
Mariko has strong preferences,

232
00:15:32,364 --> 00:15:35,100
so she isn't causing trouble for everyone
in the Brand Business Department, is she?

233
00:15:35,167 --> 00:15:36,502
Not at all.

234
00:15:36,568 --> 00:15:38,304
Everyone is motivated and working hard.

235
00:15:39,038 --> 00:15:40,406
-Is that so?
-Yes.

236
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:44,476
So this is the young employee
you played golf with

237
00:15:44,543 --> 00:15:46,045
the other day.

238
00:15:46,845 --> 00:15:48,614
Yukimura is very good at golf.

239
00:15:48,681 --> 00:15:50,149
He's also polite.

240
00:15:50,215 --> 00:15:51,383
Let's go again soon.

241
00:15:51,450 --> 00:15:52,885
I would be honored.

242
00:15:55,821 --> 00:15:57,990
When did you join Mariko's department?

243
00:15:58,057 --> 00:15:59,892
I didn't know at all.

244
00:15:59,959 --> 00:16:01,360
It's only been about two weeks.

245
00:16:03,696 --> 00:16:07,700
Since Mariko selected you,
you must be very capable, huh?

246
00:16:10,369 --> 00:16:14,139
Mariko doesn't speak much,

247
00:16:14,206 --> 00:16:17,076
so you sometimes find it difficult
to know what she's thinking, right?

248
00:16:17,142 --> 00:16:19,211
No, it's not a problem.

249
00:16:20,045 --> 00:16:22,214
She has been cool since she was a child,

250
00:16:22,281 --> 00:16:25,250
and she almost never
complains or acts spoiled.

251
00:16:25,951 --> 00:16:29,288
As her father, I often don't know
what she's thinking either.

252
00:16:31,690 --> 00:16:34,994
Give Mariko your support.

253
00:16:35,060 --> 00:16:38,497
Oh, well, it would be my honor.

254
00:16:38,564 --> 00:16:40,933
You don't need to say all of that.

255
00:16:55,114 --> 00:16:57,349
-We're back.
-Hello.

256
00:16:57,416 --> 00:16:59,318
That Sota Yukimura guy you're hounding

257
00:16:59,385 --> 00:17:01,787
doesn't have much
of a presence at his company.

258
00:17:01,854 --> 00:17:03,555
Nobody knows much about him.

259
00:17:03,622 --> 00:17:07,393
All we know is he was transferred
to the Brand Department recently.

260
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:09,194
-Transferred?
-Well,

261
00:17:09,261 --> 00:17:11,330
the president's daughter
selected him specially.

262
00:17:11,397 --> 00:17:12,698
It's unprecedented.

263
00:17:14,600 --> 00:17:16,268
The president's daughter…

264
00:17:17,302 --> 00:17:20,272
The president's daughter
is Mariko Nose, right?

265
00:17:21,673 --> 00:17:22,875
Oh, well,

266
00:17:22,941 --> 00:17:25,711
there is witness information
that the victim, Tateiwa,

267
00:17:25,778 --> 00:17:27,479
and Mariko Nose
were arguing in the company

268
00:17:27,546 --> 00:17:29,481
on the day of the incident.

269
00:17:30,349 --> 00:17:33,018
Does it mean those two
being in the same department

270
00:17:33,085 --> 00:17:34,820
has some connection?

271
00:17:37,356 --> 00:17:41,193
SOTA YUKIMURA

272
00:17:52,504 --> 00:17:54,606
You seem like you like sweets.

273
00:17:54,673 --> 00:17:56,442
-I do.
-I'm glad.

274
00:17:57,543 --> 00:17:59,845
I'm going to freshen up.

275
00:17:59,912 --> 00:18:02,514
-Straight out, second door on the right.
-Okay.

276
00:18:07,119 --> 00:18:10,089
You brought someone home to us, huh?

277
00:18:10,155 --> 00:18:11,056
First time ever.

278
00:18:12,658 --> 00:18:14,460
He just came without asking.

279
00:18:19,865 --> 00:18:22,701
I'm sorry for forcing you
to come with me today.

280
00:18:22,768 --> 00:18:23,836
Not at all.

281
00:18:24,970 --> 00:18:25,838
I enjoyed it.

282
00:18:26,939 --> 00:18:28,340
Liar.

283
00:18:29,241 --> 00:18:30,509
You actually hated it, didn't you?

284
00:18:31,543 --> 00:18:35,781
I didn't hate it,
I was just quite nervous.

285
00:18:36,915 --> 00:18:38,150
Of course you were.

286
00:18:38,817 --> 00:18:40,052
I'm sorry.

287
00:18:41,887 --> 00:18:44,790
But you fit in surprisingly well.

288
00:18:45,657 --> 00:18:46,825
Me?

289
00:18:46,892 --> 00:18:48,660
I was really out of place.

290
00:18:48,727 --> 00:18:51,296
No, you fit in better than me.

291
00:18:56,268 --> 00:18:58,237
You are a mysterious person.

292
00:19:00,772 --> 00:19:02,808
Like someone who always
has perfect timing.

293
00:19:05,110 --> 00:19:08,580
You give me what I want, when I want it.

294
00:19:08,647 --> 00:19:11,116
You appear at the perfect time.

295
00:19:12,718 --> 00:19:13,785
Really?

296
00:19:15,254 --> 00:19:16,088
Yes.

297
00:19:20,859 --> 00:19:22,127
Like a wall.

298
00:19:25,063 --> 00:19:27,399
I just notice that you're there.

299
00:19:27,466 --> 00:19:28,967
But whether you're actually there or not

300
00:19:29,902 --> 00:19:30,969
doesn't really bother me.

301
00:19:35,974 --> 00:19:37,009
Sorry.

302
00:19:37,943 --> 00:19:39,878
Does "sorry" mean that was an insult?

303
00:19:39,945 --> 00:19:42,514
No, that's not what I meant at all.

304
00:19:47,452 --> 00:19:49,755
Is the birthday party an annual tradition?

305
00:19:51,290 --> 00:19:52,157
Yes.

306
00:19:53,559 --> 00:19:56,695
It's easy to just refuse to go, but…

307
00:19:58,964 --> 00:19:59,798
Actually…

308
00:20:01,667 --> 00:20:05,037
when I was a kid, I hated it so much
that I ran away from home.

309
00:20:08,407 --> 00:20:09,474
But today,

310
00:20:10,275 --> 00:20:12,244
I've decided to definitely go home

311
00:20:12,311 --> 00:20:14,046
because I want to celebrate my mother.

312
00:20:14,846 --> 00:20:16,415
Your mother?

313
00:20:16,481 --> 00:20:17,382
Yes.

314
00:20:19,751 --> 00:20:24,890
A birthday is the day a mother worked hard
to give birth to her child, right?

315
00:20:26,458 --> 00:20:28,927
If anything, that is more precious.

316
00:20:30,562 --> 00:20:31,430
So, the gathering earlier

317
00:20:32,798 --> 00:20:34,233
was a celebration for my mother.

318
00:20:39,137 --> 00:20:41,006
You really are kind, Mariko.

319
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:42,941
What, how?

320
00:20:43,742 --> 00:20:45,677
I just want to put pressure on my father.

321
00:20:46,411 --> 00:20:48,080
To show him that I won't forgive him
if he forgets my mother.

322
00:20:50,315 --> 00:20:51,383
It's a spiteful way of thinking.

323
00:20:53,118 --> 00:20:57,222
My dad probably thinks
it's annoying deep down too.

324
00:20:57,289 --> 00:20:58,790
I don't think so.

325
00:21:00,192 --> 00:21:04,329
Because every year, only on this day,
he comes home without scheduling any work.

326
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:08,300
He just really wants
to celebrate your birthday.

327
00:21:10,602 --> 00:21:12,571
How do you know something like that?

328
00:21:14,806 --> 00:21:15,741
Ah…

329
00:21:17,142 --> 00:21:20,312
I just thought that must be the case.

330
00:21:22,281 --> 00:21:23,148
Wait a second.

331
00:21:30,822 --> 00:21:32,157
Thank you for waiting.

332
00:21:32,824 --> 00:21:35,527
-Thank you.
-Thank you very much.

333
00:21:41,667 --> 00:21:42,501
Um…

334
00:21:44,102 --> 00:21:45,003
Here.

335
00:21:47,205 --> 00:21:48,974
I didn't get you a birthday present, so…

336
00:21:51,810 --> 00:21:55,113
-I'm sorry. I've overstepped.
-Oh, no.

337
00:21:56,748 --> 00:21:57,616
Thank you.

338
00:22:06,024 --> 00:22:08,327
Is the gathering
the only thing you do to celebrate?

339
00:22:10,495 --> 00:22:11,596
Yes.

340
00:22:12,431 --> 00:22:15,400
In the first place, I hate birthdays.

341
00:22:17,369 --> 00:22:18,837
I'm not good with events and things.

342
00:22:21,273 --> 00:22:22,140
I see.

343
00:22:33,118 --> 00:22:34,853
<i>Today is Mariko's birthday.</i>

344
00:22:36,455 --> 00:22:39,224
<i>I also attended the annual birthday party</i>
<i>held at her family's house</i>

345
00:22:40,025 --> 00:22:42,427
<i>this year.</i>

346
00:23:00,746 --> 00:23:02,080
<i>Ever since that year,</i>

347
00:23:02,981 --> 00:23:05,717
<i>Mariko's always hated her birthday.</i>

348
00:23:48,427 --> 00:23:51,463
So this is your spot, huh?

349
00:23:51,530 --> 00:23:53,298
Yes. I've been coming a lot lately.

350
00:23:53,365 --> 00:23:54,299
Wow…

351
00:23:54,366 --> 00:23:55,700
Oh, Mariko, welcome.

352
00:23:55,767 --> 00:23:57,702
-Oh…
-Oh…

353
00:23:59,704 --> 00:24:01,006
Hello.

354
00:24:03,708 --> 00:24:07,612
Mariko and Yukimura
went to the same university?

355
00:24:07,679 --> 00:24:09,881
Yes, it feels like
a fateful reunion, doesn't it?

356
00:24:09,948 --> 00:24:11,850
Well, I don't know about fate.

357
00:24:11,917 --> 00:24:14,052
How long have you been running this shop?

358
00:24:14,119 --> 00:24:16,388
Since last autumn. Almost a year now.

359
00:24:16,455 --> 00:24:19,024
Wow, I didn't notice it at all.

360
00:24:19,090 --> 00:24:21,693
You've been coming
since I first opened, right, Mariko?

361
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:23,261
I suppose so.

362
00:24:24,162 --> 00:24:25,063
What?

363
00:24:25,564 --> 00:24:30,769
Does that mean you knew Mariko
all this time, but never spoke to her?

364
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:33,238
-Isn't that creepy?
-What? That'd be scary.

365
00:24:33,805 --> 00:24:34,940
I was being considerate.

366
00:24:35,006 --> 00:24:38,043
But Mariko didn't know, right?

367
00:24:38,109 --> 00:24:40,846
To be known one-sidedly…

368
00:24:40,912 --> 00:24:43,281
Wouldn't you want him
to just speak to you already?

369
00:24:44,716 --> 00:24:48,820
No, um, I was glad he left me alone.

370
00:24:49,387 --> 00:24:52,257
If he had spoken to me,
I probably would have stopped coming.

371
00:24:52,324 --> 00:24:54,559
No way, what? But it's okay now, right?

372
00:24:56,294 --> 00:24:57,462
Yes, it's fine.

373
00:24:57,529 --> 00:25:00,265
Oh, thank goodness. That startled me.

374
00:25:00,332 --> 00:25:02,334
I thought Sota would get mad at me.

375
00:25:02,901 --> 00:25:04,035
What? Yukimura?

376
00:25:05,103 --> 00:25:07,339
Oh, no, uh, just talking to myself, yes.

377
00:25:09,207 --> 00:25:12,043
-Excuse me.
-Oh, yes? I'm coming.

378
00:25:12,110 --> 00:25:13,879
Well then, enjoy.

379
00:25:15,146 --> 00:25:16,681
-One person, right?
-Yes.

380
00:25:18,250 --> 00:25:21,319
He's Yukimura's friend, right?

381
00:25:21,887 --> 00:25:24,856
-They're pretty different…
-Yes.

382
00:25:25,590 --> 00:25:27,425
But he doesn't seem like a bad person.

383
00:25:29,060 --> 00:25:30,262
That's true.

384
00:25:42,340 --> 00:25:43,608
Manager.

385
00:25:43,675 --> 00:25:45,844
Please take a look at this.

386
00:25:45,911 --> 00:25:48,580
Just leave those. I'll check later.

387
00:25:50,849 --> 00:25:52,884
Izumi, um…

388
00:25:52,951 --> 00:25:54,119
What is it?

389
00:25:54,185 --> 00:25:55,020
Did you have a fight?

390
00:25:55,587 --> 00:25:56,788
No.

391
00:25:57,756 --> 00:26:00,225
It's not that. It's just that
the Keihin Department Store samples…

392
00:26:00,292 --> 00:26:02,494
This is bad! Mariko!

393
00:26:02,561 --> 00:26:03,862
-Its really bad!
-What's wrong?

394
00:26:04,763 --> 00:26:08,600
Yukimura is suspected in Tateiwa's case.

395
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:10,068
What?

396
00:26:10,135 --> 00:26:13,438
It seems the police
are questioning our employees.

397
00:26:13,505 --> 00:26:17,008
Several people said
they were asked about Yukimura.

398
00:26:25,917 --> 00:26:28,086
-Sorry for doing this during work hours.
-No.

399
00:26:28,787 --> 00:26:30,455
I haven't taken my lunch break yet.

400
00:26:30,522 --> 00:26:31,623
It's perfect timing.

401
00:26:32,958 --> 00:26:34,059
Have a seat.

402
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:38,029
Since I didn't hear from you,

403
00:26:38,797 --> 00:26:40,865
I thought I might have been rejected.

404
00:26:41,399 --> 00:26:44,869
I'm sorry. I've been very busy
with some things.

405
00:26:45,937 --> 00:26:48,173
Your work seems busy.

406
00:26:50,208 --> 00:26:52,577
There is one thing I wanted to ask.

407
00:26:52,644 --> 00:26:55,580
Yes, ask me anything.

408
00:26:57,649 --> 00:27:01,753
Is it true that an employee of ours
is suspected in Tateiwa's case?

409
00:27:05,890 --> 00:27:08,827
Man, so that's what this is about…

410
00:27:11,730 --> 00:27:15,600
The reason you agreed to have a meal
with me is because I'm a police officer.

411
00:27:15,667 --> 00:27:16,835
Oh, no, um…

412
00:27:20,672 --> 00:27:21,506
I'm sorry.

413
00:27:23,675 --> 00:27:24,909
You are a straightforward person.

414
00:27:26,444 --> 00:27:29,047
Since it's under investigation,
I can't speak in detail,

415
00:27:30,415 --> 00:27:33,318
but we are looking
at various possibilities.

416
00:27:33,385 --> 00:27:36,855
I am truly sorry about Tateiwa.

417
00:27:37,689 --> 00:27:40,892
But it is impossible
that the culprit is my subordinate.

418
00:27:41,826 --> 00:27:43,495
I didn't mention this,

419
00:27:43,561 --> 00:27:46,364
but I was having a dispute
with Mr. Tateiwa over work,

420
00:27:46,431 --> 00:27:48,733
and we had an argument
right before the incident.

421
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:52,137
Isn't it because of that
my subordinates are suspected?

422
00:27:52,203 --> 00:27:56,608
Or do the police think my subordinates
also had a bad relationship with Tateiwa?

423
00:27:57,575 --> 00:27:59,778
It was my personal issue.

424
00:27:59,844 --> 00:28:01,346
My subordinates have nothing
to do with it.

425
00:28:03,581 --> 00:28:06,317
We just need to investigate
from all angles,

426
00:28:06,918 --> 00:28:08,586
so please don't worry.

427
00:28:11,056 --> 00:28:13,792
Of course. I'm sorry.

428
00:28:16,161 --> 00:28:18,797
You are a good boss, Mariko.

429
00:28:20,732 --> 00:28:24,903
To be so desperate
to protect your subordinates…

430
00:28:34,245 --> 00:28:35,880
It's just as your grandmother said.

431
00:28:37,449 --> 00:28:39,284
Mariko isn't very sociable,

432
00:28:40,385 --> 00:28:41,986
but she is a kind child.

433
00:28:43,088 --> 00:28:45,190
She told me that many times before we met.

434
00:28:46,825 --> 00:28:48,126
She said that?

435
00:28:50,128 --> 00:28:51,763
You're a lot like your mother.

436
00:28:54,299 --> 00:28:56,401
She was a quiet person,

437
00:28:58,369 --> 00:28:59,771
but she was like a sunflower

438
00:29:00,972 --> 00:29:02,574
blooming in the family.

439
00:29:06,344 --> 00:29:08,747
You're exactly like her.

440
00:29:13,284 --> 00:29:14,919
My grandmother told you all that?

441
00:29:17,722 --> 00:29:19,691
You might have heard,

442
00:29:19,758 --> 00:29:24,596
but my mother died
in an accident when I was seven.

443
00:29:30,468 --> 00:29:33,204
That must have been very sad for you.

444
00:29:34,939 --> 00:29:37,575
I'm not sure what to say…

445
00:29:37,642 --> 00:29:39,344
Oh, no, it's okay.

446
00:29:40,278 --> 00:29:42,380
I'm a full adult now.

447
00:29:43,648 --> 00:29:45,183
It's something from the past.

448
00:29:53,424 --> 00:29:55,994
How about finding a new place?

449
00:29:57,328 --> 00:30:01,099
Instead of the past, creating a new future

450
00:30:02,367 --> 00:30:04,636
might be better for you.

451
00:30:07,572 --> 00:30:09,407
That was weird, wasn't it…

452
00:30:09,474 --> 00:30:11,142
-Oh, no…
-I'm sorry.

453
00:30:12,844 --> 00:30:15,213
<i>I want Mariko to smile.</i>

454
00:30:19,350 --> 00:30:22,887
<i>If that smile were to belong</i>
<i>to someone else…</i>

455
00:30:33,565 --> 00:30:35,366
NEW ASSETS

456
00:30:39,204 --> 00:30:40,205
HELLO, THIS IS KIKUTA.

457
00:30:40,271 --> 00:30:41,940
I SHOULD BE ABLE TO REPORT BACK
ON THE MATTER YOU REQUESTED TOMORROW.

458
00:30:42,006 --> 00:30:44,342
KIKUTA PRIVATE INVESTIGATION

459
00:30:45,109 --> 00:30:46,244
What?

460
00:30:47,645 --> 00:30:49,013
A detective agency?

461
00:31:05,263 --> 00:31:06,865
Good work, everyone. I'm heading out.

462
00:31:06,931 --> 00:31:08,466
-Goodbye.
-Thank you.

463
00:31:11,302 --> 00:31:12,971
FATHER

464
00:31:14,172 --> 00:31:15,073
Yes?

465
00:31:17,675 --> 00:31:18,543
Understood.

466
00:31:19,077 --> 00:31:19,911
Okay.

467
00:31:23,248 --> 00:31:24,115
Excuse me.

468
00:31:24,816 --> 00:31:27,919
Sorry, this isn't about work.

469
00:31:33,791 --> 00:31:34,626
What is it?

470
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:36,995
Sayuri asked me to give this to you.

471
00:31:44,936 --> 00:31:46,571
This is Mom's…

472
00:31:47,171 --> 00:31:49,274
She had it repaired to fit your size.

473
00:31:50,141 --> 00:31:51,809
Contact her and thank her.

474
00:32:02,086 --> 00:32:03,554
I didn't ask for this…

475
00:32:06,758 --> 00:32:07,926
I didn't ask for this!

476
00:32:08,493 --> 00:32:10,261
Why would she do this without asking?

477
00:32:11,029 --> 00:32:13,731
Why is she touching Mom's things
without permission?

478
00:32:15,533 --> 00:32:17,535
Sayuri was only thinking of you--

479
00:32:17,602 --> 00:32:20,071
I'm saying that it's selfish!

480
00:32:24,842 --> 00:32:26,010
Mariko.

481
00:32:35,720 --> 00:32:36,988
Ah, manager.

482
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:41,693
What's wrong?

483
00:32:42,527 --> 00:32:43,428
It's nothing.

484
00:33:07,752 --> 00:33:09,754
<i>Happy birthday.</i>

485
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:10,688
<i>Happy birthday, Mariko.</i>

486
00:33:13,524 --> 00:33:14,425
This is wrong.

487
00:33:17,028 --> 00:33:18,730
This is all wrong!

488
00:33:19,864 --> 00:33:20,832
Mariko!

489
00:33:31,309 --> 00:33:32,310
Mom…

490
00:33:37,281 --> 00:33:38,716
<i>Mom…</i>

491
00:34:05,243 --> 00:34:06,277
Mariko…

492
00:34:07,812 --> 00:34:08,713
Why?

493
00:34:10,915 --> 00:34:12,183
I felt like you were crying.

494
00:34:16,821 --> 00:34:17,922
What?

495
00:34:19,323 --> 00:34:21,259
What are you saying?

496
00:34:21,859 --> 00:34:23,428
Wait, how did you know I was here?

497
00:34:26,397 --> 00:34:28,132
I just happened to be passing by.

498
00:34:28,199 --> 00:34:29,367
You just happened to?

499
00:34:30,068 --> 00:34:31,669
You're completely out of breath.

500
00:34:37,742 --> 00:34:39,310
What are you?

501
00:34:41,446 --> 00:34:43,648
Giving me the flower my mother liked

502
00:34:44,315 --> 00:34:45,750
and even knowing this park…

503
00:34:52,156 --> 00:34:53,524
This is a special place, isn't it?

504
00:34:59,163 --> 00:35:00,031
This is…

505
00:35:02,467 --> 00:35:04,102
my sanctuary.

506
00:35:09,173 --> 00:35:10,341
When I was a kid,

507
00:35:12,877 --> 00:35:15,113
whenever something bad happened,
I always came here.

508
00:35:17,949 --> 00:35:20,151
Because when I was here,
Mom would come to get me.

509
00:35:24,722 --> 00:35:26,324
The year my mother died…

510
00:35:29,293 --> 00:35:31,362
I ran away from home
because I hated the birthday party.

511
00:35:34,265 --> 00:35:35,266
Alone.

512
00:35:39,837 --> 00:35:43,207
Even though I knew Mom
wouldn't come to get me anymore.

513
00:35:48,379 --> 00:35:49,614
But strangely…

514
00:35:51,582 --> 00:35:56,254
being here made me feel
like Mom was nearby,

515
00:35:58,923 --> 00:35:59,957
and I was able

516
00:36:01,726 --> 00:36:03,561
to return home safely

517
00:36:04,462 --> 00:36:05,730
without losing my way.

518
00:36:13,271 --> 00:36:14,505
Wait…

519
00:36:14,572 --> 00:36:16,707
Why am I telling you this?

520
00:36:23,181 --> 00:36:24,549
You hate birthdays

521
00:36:26,384 --> 00:36:27,952
because they remind you of your mother.

522
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:31,622
You hate the birthday parties

523
00:36:33,157 --> 00:36:35,960
because you loved the birthday parties

524
00:36:37,595 --> 00:36:38,930
your mother threw for you.

525
00:36:43,935 --> 00:36:45,036
My father is right.

526
00:36:46,837 --> 00:36:48,306
I'm acting like a fool at my age.

527
00:36:48,372 --> 00:36:50,107
I shouldn't even worry about it.

528
00:36:50,174 --> 00:36:51,108
That doesn't matter.

529
00:36:54,178 --> 00:36:55,746
No matter how old you get…

530
00:36:59,016 --> 00:37:01,052
or how many years pass…

531
00:37:03,054 --> 00:37:04,889
you cannot forget sad things.

532
00:37:07,725 --> 00:37:09,026
You just get better…

533
00:37:11,329 --> 00:37:12,830
at pretending to forget,

534
00:37:14,065 --> 00:37:15,700
better at lying that you forgot.

535
00:37:19,303 --> 00:37:22,974
I don't think you need to force yourself

536
00:37:24,809 --> 00:37:26,410
to share your true feelings

537
00:37:27,912 --> 00:37:29,780
or show your weakness to anyone.

538
00:37:31,215 --> 00:37:33,150
I think there are people
who are not good at that.

539
00:37:36,420 --> 00:37:37,288
But…

540
00:37:41,025 --> 00:37:43,294
please just at least
don't lie to yourself.

541
00:37:47,031 --> 00:37:48,566
The sadness…

542
00:37:50,234 --> 00:37:51,502
of losing your mother…

543
00:37:53,137 --> 00:37:54,305
the loneliness…

544
00:37:57,508 --> 00:38:00,745
Even the happiness of the birthday parties
she threw for you when you were a child…

545
00:38:03,648 --> 00:38:07,218
Please don't seal them away
and pretend they didn't exist.

546
00:38:11,422 --> 00:38:14,792
Please, keep them with you, always.

547
00:38:24,802 --> 00:38:26,437
I've never cried
in front of someone before.

548
00:38:29,607 --> 00:38:31,375
I'm a wall. It's okay.

549
00:38:34,145 --> 00:38:35,413
I'm just here.

550
00:38:37,715 --> 00:38:38,816
So don't worry.

551
00:38:41,018 --> 00:38:41,919
What is that?

552
00:39:50,955 --> 00:39:51,922
<i>This is strange.</i>

553
00:39:52,823 --> 00:39:55,092
<i>I thought I only wanted</i>
<i>to see Mariko smile, but…</i>

554
00:40:00,431 --> 00:40:01,665
Mom…

555
00:40:06,103 --> 00:40:07,772
That white one…

556
00:40:07,838 --> 00:40:11,041
The white cylindrical one
that's big at the top.

557
00:40:11,108 --> 00:40:13,444
Is it this one?

558
00:40:13,511 --> 00:40:15,613
No, not that. It's about this big…

559
00:40:44,442 --> 00:40:45,409
Mom?

560
00:41:11,802 --> 00:41:13,537
"I HELD A GRUDGE AGAINST THE ENTIRE WORLD"

561
00:41:13,604 --> 00:41:15,139
It's a good article.

562
00:41:15,873 --> 00:41:17,708
I hear it's selling pretty well.

563
00:41:18,542 --> 00:41:21,078
But they paid me almost nothing.

564
00:41:22,847 --> 00:41:24,348
This is you?

565
00:41:24,415 --> 00:41:26,283
It's a tough world, huh?

566
00:41:28,452 --> 00:41:30,821
I wonder if I have any other stories
that would sell for more.

567
00:41:36,260 --> 00:41:38,796
<i>A single factor</i>
<i>that exerts power is strong.</i>

568
00:41:40,130 --> 00:41:41,932
<i>-But…</i>
-…you like sweets.

569
00:41:41,999 --> 00:41:43,834
-I do.
-I'm glad.

570
00:41:45,135 --> 00:41:47,271
I'm going to freshen up.

571
00:41:47,338 --> 00:41:50,140
-Straight out, second door on the right.
-Okay.

572
00:41:55,513 --> 00:41:57,147
What time are you coming home?

573
00:41:58,816 --> 00:41:59,884
Keito?

574
00:42:02,152 --> 00:42:03,754
Hey, Keito!

575
00:42:03,821 --> 00:42:07,558
<i>When multiple factors intertwine,</i>
<i>it becomes complex and troublesome.</i>

576
00:42:24,708 --> 00:42:25,576
No.

577
00:42:26,243 --> 00:42:28,178
Because I don't want
my daughter to hear about it.

578
00:42:29,113 --> 00:42:30,514
Yes.

579
00:42:30,581 --> 00:42:35,019
Talking again after getting out of prison,
he really is a plague.

580
00:42:37,421 --> 00:42:38,656
Thank you.

581
00:42:39,490 --> 00:42:40,391
Goodbye.

582
00:43:16,527 --> 00:43:18,596
AWARD

583
00:43:53,097 --> 00:43:54,031
Whoa…

584
00:44:03,507 --> 00:44:05,809
<i>There is still something in this family.</i>

585
00:44:06,777 --> 00:44:08,979
<i>Something that steals Mariko's smile.</i>

586
00:44:09,046 --> 00:44:10,814
PRESIDENT'S OFFICE

587
00:44:11,548 --> 00:44:13,384
<i>Is the risk factor an individual?</i>

588
00:44:14,051 --> 00:44:16,820
-<i>Or is it…</i>
-Yukimura.

589
00:44:19,790 --> 00:44:21,158
I need to talk to you.

590
00:44:53,157 --> 00:44:55,292
<i>I want Mariko to be happy.</i>

591
00:44:56,393 --> 00:44:57,461
<i>That's all.</i>

592
00:44:59,029 --> 00:45:01,598
What are you to Mariko?

593
00:45:01,665 --> 00:45:03,467
NEXT TIME…

594
00:45:08,038 --> 00:45:08,972
Subtitle translation by: Maslin Friedman
